      concentration on the host, not close.",
  potential_win: "One Python interpreter per node carries real baseline
      memory/CPU overhead x83 instances -- consolidating (fewer
      processes, or a Rust rewrite) would be a measurable footprint
      reduction. Also likely relevant to I/O contention specifically: if
      each node writes its own .jsonl log continuously (matches
      topic-clima-phi-up's own description -- 'also logged at
      /var/log/vsf-clima/*.phi_up.jsonl on LXC 112'), that is 87 separate
      write streams competing for the same disk, not one -- a real
      multiplier, not just a memory question.",
  the_real_tension_do_not_skip: "One-process-per-node may not be an
      implementation accident -- it plausibly reflects the recursive VSM
      design intent itself (each L3 node as a proto-independent viable
      system, not just an aggregated data point -- matches the earlier
      finding this session that 'L3 nodes are proto-VSMs already', see
      ITEM-21-DESIGN). Consolidating processes for efficiency could
      undermine the genuine per-node autonomy property the experiment is
      actually testing. This needs real architectural thought before any
      migration, not a quick rewrite -- study the tradeoff explicitly,
